  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "First published September 1899 by John Stark & Son, Sedalia, Missouri; Joplin died in 1917, so the rag is free in the US and under life-plus-70 alike.",
    "sourceEdition": "Joplin, 'Maple Leaf Rag', John Stark & Son 1899 piano score, read through two public-domain ABC transcriptions of the first and second strains (John Chambers, 2018). The two readings agree note for note everywhere except the arpeggio sweep at A-strain bars 7-8, where one repeats a two-octave sweep twice and the other climbs continuously; the continuous climb is encoded here.",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only; the rag is instrumental and carries no lyrics. Joplin published it in A-flat; encoded here in F, the key of the public-domain transcriptions read - the tune is identical and LoopStar transposes freely. Written in 2/4 with 16th-note figures and encoded as 4/4 with each written bar becoming one encoded bar and every value doubled, the standard cut-time mapping, so tempoBPM 168 is a written quarter of 84. Ragtime is straight, never triplet. The bar-granular chord grid takes the primary chord where the score changes mid-bar, so the chromatic F - E7 - Eb walk inside one bar of the B strain is flattened to its opening chord. The trio strains are not carried in v1. Confidence is medium because the encoding rests on transcriptions rather than the engraved score."
  },
